-- title:
--   Equal Xi-image over an Artinian base: compatible isomorphisms
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ and $\bar r$ are primes with $\bar r\neq r$, and $N$ is a non-zero natural number with $r\nmid N$.
--
--   **Coefficient rings.** $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), $\pi\in\mathcal O$ is irreducible, $\mathcal O$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete, its residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements, and $(r)=(\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (so $\mathcal O$ is absolutely unramified over $\mathbb Z_r$); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **The unramified layer.** $\mathcal O^{nr}$ (`Onr`) is a characteristic-zero domain and an $\mathcal O$-algebra, with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: $\mathcal O^{nr}$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $\mathcal O^{nr}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo that ideal (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $\mathcal O^{nr}$ of positive degree has a root modulo that ideal (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r}$ modulo that ideal for all $x$ (`hFr`), i.e. $\mathrm{Fr}$ is a Frobenius lift.
--
--   **Quaternionic data.** For $a,b\in\mathbb Q$, the hypothesis `hB` asserts that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is indefinite ($0<a$ or $0<b$) and that, for a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$, the $v$-adic completion of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies over $r$ or over $\bar r$. $\Lambda$ is a $\mathbb Z$-submodule of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ which is a maximal order (an order, maximal among orders containing it), and which contains every rational integer (`hΛℤ`). The map $\mathrm{coord}\colon\Lambda\to\mathbb Z_{r^2}\times\mathbb Z_{r^2}$ (with $\mathbb Z_{r^2}=$ `Zp2 r` $=$ Witt vectors of $\mathbb F_{r^2}$) satisfies `IsOrderCoord`: it is additive, sends $1$ to $(1,0)$, is multiplicative for the twisted rule $(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)\cdot(\beta_1,\beta_2)=(\alpha_1\beta_1+r\,\alpha_2\,\varphi(\beta_2),\ \alpha_1\beta_2+\alpha_2\varphi(\beta_1))$ with $\varphi$ the Witt-vector Frobenius, is injective, has $r$-adically dense image in each coordinate, and satisfies $\mathrm{coord}(m)_1+\varphi(\mathrm{coord}(m)_1)=n$ whenever $m+\bar m=n\in\mathbb Z$.
--
--   **Base point.** $A_0$ is a fake elliptic curve over $\mathcal O^{nr}/(\pi)$ for $(\Lambda,N)$, $X_0$ is a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $\mathcal O^{nr}/(\pi)$ of height $4$, $\theta_0$ is a system of formal coordinates in two variables for $A_0.f$, and `hA₀` asserts that $\theta_0$ realises $X_0$ as the formal group of $A_0$ compatibly with $\mathrm{coord}$ (the clause `IsFormalModuleVia`: $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates for the relative group law, and the $\Lambda$-action on formal points is computed by the series $\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{coord}(m)_1)+\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{coord}(m)_2)\circ\varpi$).
--
--   **Drinfeld's datum.** $\iota\colon\mathbb Z_{r^2}\to\mathcal O^{nr}$ is a ring homomorphism; $\Phi$ is a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $\mathcal O^{nr}/(r)$ which is special relative to the map induced by $\iota$ (the Lie algebra splits into the two eigenparts, each an invertible module) and has height $4$. $M$ is a moduli package over $(r,\mathcal O^{nr})$ satisfying the Zariski sheaf condition `hM` (separatedness and gluing for finite covers by distinguished opens). The family $\eta$ assigns, to every commutative ring $B$ with a map $\psi\colon\mathcal O^{nr}\to B$ and $r$ nilpotent in $B$, a map from rigidified objects `Rigidified r Φ B` to $M.\mathrm{obj}\,B$. The hypothesis `hη` has three clauses: for Noetherian $B$, $\eta$ identifies two $\iota$-admissible rigidified objects exactly when they are isomorphic; $\eta$ is natural for ring maps over $\mathcal O^{nr}$ on admissible objects; and every point of $M.\mathrm{obj}\,B$ is, after passing to the members of a finite cover of $B$ by localisations generating the unit ideal, the $\eta$-image of an admissible rigidified object.
--
--   **Endomorphism comparison.** $E_0$ is a ring homomorphism from the centraliser of $\{\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}\,a\}_a\cup\{\Phi.\mathrm{varpiEnd}\}$ into $M_2(K_0)$, and `hE₀` asserts that $E_0$ is injective and that for some $m$ the image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal O)$ in the precise sense that $r^{m}$ times the image of every matrix over $\mathcal O$ lies in the image of $E_0$, and $r^{m}\,E_0(e)$ comes from a matrix over $\mathcal O$ for every $e$.
--
--   **Reduction comparison.** A ring homomorphism $\kappa\colon\mathcal O^{nr}/(\pi)\to\mathcal O^{nr}/(r)$ compatible with the two quotient maps (`hκ`), a natural number $n_0$ and a system of series $\beta_0$ over $\mathcal O^{nr}/(r)$ which is an isogeny $\Phi\to X_0\otimes_\kappa$ of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`).
--
--   **The dictionary $\Xi$.** $\Xi$ assigns, to every Noetherian commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ and $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi\colon\mathcal O^{nr}\to B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent in $B$, a map from rigidified fake elliptic curves $(E,\rho)$ over $B$ with leg $\psi$ to $\mathcal O$-points `ModuliPackage.GPoint 𝒪 M B` (a leg $\mathcal O^{nr}\to B$, nilpotence of $r$, and a point of $M$). Four hypotheses constrain $\Xi$. `hΞleg`: over a $B$ whose idempotents are trivial, the leg of $\Xi(x)$ is $\mathrm{frobTwist}\,\mathcal O^{nr}\,\mathrm{Fr}\,k\,\psi=\psi\circ\mathrm{Fr}^{k}$ for some $k\in\mathbb Z$. `hΞnat`: if $\varphi\colon B\to B'$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra map, $g$ exhibits $x'.1$ as the pullback of $x.1$ along $\varphi$ in the sense of `FakeEllipticCurve.IsPullbackVia` (a scheme-theoretic pullback square compatible with the group laws, the $\Lambda$-action and level structures), and the rigidifications correspond under `Rigidification.IsPullbackVia`, then $\Xi_{B'}(x')=(\Xi_B(x)).\mathrm{map}\,\varphi$. `hΞiso`: $\Xi_B(x)=\Xi_B(x')$ whenever there is an isomorphism $i\colon x.1.A\cong x'.1.A$ over $B$ with $i\circ$ structure map compatibility and `IsoVia` (compatibility with group law, $\Lambda$-action and level structure), together with maps $i_b$ of the reductions and $u_A$ of the $A_0$-pullbacks, compatible with $g_b$, the structure maps and $g_A$, and exponents $i_1,j_1$ for which $i_b$ followed by $x'.2.\varphi$, by $u_A$ and by the action of $r^{i_1}$ agrees with $x.2.\varphi$ followed by the action of $r^{j_1}$. `hΞdef`: over a $B$ with trivial idempotents and $r$ nilpotent, if $x$ carries a formal module structure $(X,\theta)$ via $\mathrm{coord}$, then there are $j\le 1$ and a rigidified object $t$ over $B$ with $t.X=X$, with $\mathrm{IsRigTransport}\,\theta_0\,\kappa\,\beta_0\,x.2\,\theta\,j\,t$, with $t$ admissible for $\iota$ and the leg $\psi\circ\mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$, and $\Xi_B(x)=\langle\psi\circ\mathrm{Fr}^{-j},\ \eta_B(t)\rangle$.
--
--   **The Artinian base.** $A$ is an Artinian local commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, $\kappa$ is an algebraically closed field which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent (the binder name repeats that of the reduction map above), $q_\kappa\colon A\to\kappa$ is a surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra map whose kernel is the maximal ideal of $A$, $\psi\colon\mathcal O^{nr}\to A$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra map, and $r$ is nilpotent in $A$ (`hAr`).
--
--   **The two curves.** $x,x'$ are rigidified fake elliptic curves over $A$ with leg $\psi$; $X,X'$ are formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules over $A$ and $\theta,\theta'$ formal coordinates for $x.1.f$, $x'.1.f$ with `hX`, `hX'` asserting that $x.1$, $x'.1$ are formal modules via $\mathrm{coord}$ for $(X,\theta)$, $(X',\theta')$. Further, $x_0,x_0'$ are rigidified curves over $\kappa$ with leg $q_\kappa\circ\psi$, and $g\colon x_0.1.A\to x.1.A$, $g'\colon x_0'.1.A\to x'.1.A$ exhibit them as the pullbacks of $x.1$, $x'.1$ along $q_\kappa$ (`hg`, `hg'`), the rigidifications corresponding (`hρ`, `hρ'`). Finally $\Xi_A(x)=\Xi_A(x')$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist: an isomorphism $e_0\colon x_0.1.A\cong x_0'.1.A$ with $e_0$ followed by $x_0'.1.f$ equal to $x_0.1.f$, and with $\mathrm{IsoVia}$, that is, $e_0$ carries the group law of $x_0.1$ to that of $x_0'.1$, commutes with the $\Lambda$-actions, and matches the level structures in both directions; a morphism $i_{b,0}\colon x_0.2.E_b.A\to x_0'.2.E_b.A$ with $i_{b,0}$ followed by $x_0'.2.g_b$ equal to $x_0.2.g_b$ followed by $e_0$, and $i_{b,0}$ followed by $x_0'.2.E_b.f$ equal to $x_0.2.E_b.f$; a morphism $u_{A,0}\colon x_0'.2.A_b.A\to x_0.2.A_b.A$ which exhibits $x_0'.2.A_b$ as the pullback of $x_0.2.A_b$ along the identity, and with $u_{A,0}$ followed by $x_0.2.g_A$ equal to $x_0'.2.g_A$; natural numbers $i_0,j_0$ such that $i_{b,0}$ followed by $x_0'.2.\varphi$, by $u_{A,0}$ and by the action of $r^{i_0}\in\Lambda$ on $x_0.2.A_b$ equals $x_0.2.\varphi$ followed by the action of $r^{j_0}$; and a homomorphism $T\colon X\to X'$ of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules over $A$ which is an isomorphism (it has a two-sided inverse homomorphism).
--
--   These are moreover compatible in the following sense: for every commutative ring $B''$ which is both an $A$-algebra and a $\kappa$-algebra with $\mathrm{algebraMap}\,A\,B''=(\mathrm{algebraMap}\,\kappa\,B'')\circ q_\kappa$, every ideal $J\subseteq B''$ and $n$ with $J^{n+1}=0$, every $s\colon\mathrm{Fin}\,2\to B''$ with all $s_i\in J$, and every $p_0$, a morphism $\mathrm{Spec}\,B''\to x_0.1.A$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\kappa$, such that $p_0$ followed by $g$ is the formal point $\theta_{B''}(s)$: then $p_0$ followed by $e_0$ and then by $g'$ equals the formal point $\theta'_{B''}\bigl(i\mapsto \mathrm{nilEval}\,n\,(T_i)\,s\bigr)$, where $\mathrm{nilEval}\,n\,(T_i)\,s$ is the value at $s$ of the truncation of the $i$-th power series of $T$ in degrees at most $n$ in each variable.
--
--   This is the injectivity input of the Čerednik–Drinfeld dictionary at the level of an Artin local base: equality of the two associated points of Drinfeld's moduli package yields simultaneously an isomorphism of the reductions to the residue field, matching the rigidification data up to $r$-power multiplication, and an isomorphism of the associated formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules over the Artinian base that induces it on formal points. It feeds the Serre–Tate style lifting argument and is used in the proof that the rigidified-curves-to-$G$ map is bijective over Artin local bases with algebraically closed residue field.
